WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.620
This video is part of an epic collab at hashtag Team Trees, but more on that later.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:05.880
Hey Crazies.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.680
Most of you probably get the basic idea of photosynthesis.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.220
Plants pull water up from their roots, inhale some carbon dioxide from the air,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.720
and use energy from the Sun to power a chemical reaction.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.540
It’s basically how they eat.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.280
But, like a lot of things in biochemistry, it’s a little more complicated than that.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.360
Photosynthesis is wild.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.840
Actually, plants are not the only thing that can photosynthesize.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.500
Yeah yeah, I know.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.860
Organisms like algae and cyanobacteria can do it too.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.480
Photosynthesis has been around a lot longer than plants have.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.600
It’s just easier to talk about plants because they’re more familiar to people.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.880
Hashtag Team Trees!

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.780
Alright, some of you may not know this, but I’m married to a biologist.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.500
She helped me a lot with this video, so if I get anything wrong, it’s her fault.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.420
What was that?

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.440
It’s my fault. It’s always my fault.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.760
That’s what I thought!

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.280
Anyway, something I’ve learned from our conversations is that, in biology,

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.680
there’s always a deeper level where we can ask more questions.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.940
Think of them as various levels of abstraction.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.980
At the top, you’ll find our original explanation for photosynthesis.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.300
Plants use water, carbon dioxide, and sunlight to make food.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:26.220
Down at the bottom is the full atomic level, which is usually unnecessary.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.760
And often overwhelming to the point of uselessness.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.200
As a physicist, I like to see how deep I can go before that happens, so let’s do this.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.760
So far, photosynthesis is like one big mystery box.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.000
The water, carbon dioxide, and sunlight go in

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.360
and then glucose and oxygen come out.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.300
That glucose is a sugar and sugar equals food.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.940
But this visual is pretty misleading.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.760
It gives you the impression that, if you have a bunch of water and carbon dioxide,

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.400
and you shine some light on it,

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.420
then the molecules will just rearrange themselves,

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.840
which is false.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.000
If that were true, you could get the same reaction to happen

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.960
by just shining a light on this carbonated water.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.780
The truth is none of those molecules can absorb enough light to react like that.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.040
They need help.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.520
A lot of help.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.020
There’s a lot going on in this box.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.380
Let’s go deeper.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:23.480
In reality, the water and the carbon dioxide aren’t even used at the same stage.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.820
Stage 1 is where water and light come together to make oxygen and some energy.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.040
The oxygen is released, some of which makes it out to the atmosphere.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:38.080
The energy is passed on to stage 2, where it’s used to transform carbon dioxide

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.200
into something called a sugar precursor.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.240
It isn’t actually turned into the sugar glucose until stage 3.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.580
Technically speaking, that sugar precursor can become all sorts of sugars, not just glucose.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.820
Are you going to be on my case the whole video?!

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.560
If you keep messing up, yes.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:54.600
Ugh!

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:59.440
He’s right, as usual, but this process is going to be complicated enough.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.020
I’ve got to draw the line somewhere.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.520
So, for the rest of this video, stage 3 makes glucose.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:05.820
Just go with it.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.180
We’re going to focus most of our attention on stage 1.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.360
Because that’s where light goes in and oxygen comes out.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.840
"Photo-" means light, so we should focus on the light reactions.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.800
To understand how the heck that works, you
know where we have to go.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.100
Deeper!

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.040
The process that happens in stage 1 is called the z-scheme

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.180
because some of it’s parts kind of look like the letter Z.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:34.300
There are 5 separate parts, each taking in some particles and spitting out others.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.300
I warned you this was going to get involved, but it’s worth it, I promise.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.540
Part A splits some water into an oxygen and
a bunch of ions.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.560
That’s 4 electrons and 4 protons.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.140
Usually, those protons are labeled as ionized Hydrogen or H plus.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.020
But I’m a physicist, not a chemist.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.020
I’m just going to call it what it is: a proton.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.500
As I said before, the oxygen is simply released,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:02.480
but most of those ions are going to get used in the rest of stage 1.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.060
Part B is where the magic happens.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.820
Light comes in and gives up its energy to an electron, knocking it loose.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:14.780
One of the electrons from part A quickly falls in to fill the gap left behind.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.239
The loose electron is free to move on to part C.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.740
How the heck does that thing work?

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.100
Chlorophyll.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:23.800
Deeper!

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.720
Each plant cell is filled with little green packets called chloroplasts.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.000
Chlorophyll being inside Chloroplasts?

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.380
That’s not a coincidence.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.840
Biology is real big on prefixes and suffixes.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.100
Inside each chloroplast, there are even smaller pancake things.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.960
This is where stage 1 is actually happening.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.960
On the surface, you’ll find a lump of of various pigment molecules.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:51.780
Many of which are chlorophyll, which give plants their distinctive green color.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.980
Each of those pigments can absorb a different color of light.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.860
As you can imagine, the more light a plant
can absorb, the better off it is,

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.900
so the more variety of pigments the better.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.919
A pair of special chlorophyll hang out in the central opening.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.460
That’s where our electron is attached.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.920
Light comes in, the appropriate pigment absorbs it,

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.920
and that energy gets passed around until it gets to that middle chlorophyll.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:20.400
The electron is knocked loose and another falls in to fill the gap.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.800
The mechanism is actually kinda cool.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.380
We call it a photosystem and there are two photosystems in stage 1 of photosynthesis.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.420
So what happens with that loose electron?

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.800
It’s got some excess energy we can use.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.700
That electron hops from molecule to molecule in part C,

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.340
making biological energy along the way.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.480
Well, it’s not like it’s loose energy floating around.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:43.800
That’s not a thing.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.820
Energy is just a property.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.080
But, in biology, the word almost always refers to ATP.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:54.740
A biologically useful molecule that stores energy.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.300
This molecule is constructed from stuff already floating around nearby.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:04.360
The energy from the electron is given up to combine those parts into ATP.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.320
That’s all part C does.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:07.900
It makes ATP.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.500
Part D is another photosystem.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.639
Light comes in, an electron is knocked loose,

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.120
and the electron from part C falls in to take its place.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.440
Part E is another assembly machine.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.120
But, instead of assembling ATP, this one assembles something called NADPH.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:27.140
Biologist and their acronyms.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.720
Just think of it like a little bus that carries
electrons around.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.320
Seriously, they call it an electron bus.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.100
It just helps the cell move the electrons over to stage 2.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.120
That’s it. Nothing crazy.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:42.140
This is what stage 1 looks like overall.

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:46.140
For simplicity, we’re only showing the molecules that get moved around.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.860
Unfortunately, for stage 2 to actually work properly,

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.000
we need 3 of these to be running simultaneously.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.380
That’s the only way to give stage 2 all the energy and ions it needs to work.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.980
And that’s where the carbon dioxide comes in.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.660
Remember the carbon dioxide?

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.720
Plants can’t grow without carbon.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.480
Stage 2 happens in the main space of those green packets inside the cell.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.240
Three carbon dioxide molecules come in

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.300
along with a bunch of energy and charge from stage 1.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:20.020
Through a process called the Calvin cycle, those molecules are turned into CGP.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.220
I mean, G3P.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.140
That’s the sugar precursor we talked about earlier,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.900
which will be made into glucose in stage 3.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:29.300
Wait a minute!

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.600
It takes two of those G3P molecules to make a glucose.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:39.400
That means the cell needs six stage ones and two stage twos just to make one glucose.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:41.720
That’s forking ridiculous!

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.360
So how do photosynthesis?

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:47.719
Well, plants and other photosynthsizing organisms

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:52.140
use water, carbon dioxide, and sunlight to literally make their food.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:55.020
This process is nowhere near simple though.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.460
A whole bunch of complex chemical processes are used.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:01.360
Some molecules need to be added to the system,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.340
but others continuously cycle around.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:09.000
All just to make sugar molecules that allow them to grow and continue to exist.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:12.520
So, were you prepared for the complexity of that process?

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.020
Let us know in the comments.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.569
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:08:15.569 --> 00:08:18.040
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:21.920
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.180
This video is part of an epic collab at hashtag Team Trees.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:29.060
So, crazies, let’s talk.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.600
Global warming, climate change, whatever you want to call it.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:37.240
It’s the greatest challenge humans have ever faced and it’s our fault it’s even happening.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:39.000
Speaking of challenges,

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:44.440
back in May 2019, Reddit challenged Mr. Beast to plant 20 million trees

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.680
as a celebration for hitting 20 million subscribers.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.260
And, to literally nobody’s surprise, he was crazy enough to attempt it.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:57.040
Unfortunately, that’s a lot of trees and he can’t possibly do this by himself.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:59.500
So he’s partnered with the Arbor Day Foundation

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:04.020
and recruited hundreds of creators across multiple genres to make it happen.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:05.440
Myself included.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:10.680
Photosynthesis is still the best control we have for carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.460
so the more trees the better.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:17.780
For every dollar we donate to the Arbor Day Foundation by January 1st, 2020;

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.880
they’ll plant a single tree in a forest that needs them.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.240
One dollar equals one tree.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.640
Ten dollars equals ten trees.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.420
A hundred dollars equals a hundred trees!

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.960
Of course, that means 20 million trees is going to take 20 million dollars.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.020
Hence needing to recruit all of us to spread the word.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.540
Just so you know I’m putting my money where my mouth is,

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:41.160
I’ll be donating $100 to the cause.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.740
If you can donate, there’s a link in the doobly-doo.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.660
If you can’t donate, that’s fine too.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.500
Just share this video and others like it to spread the word.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:49.760
Let’s do this!

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.480
Hashtag Team Trees!

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.940
This was a special video today, so no featured comment.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.000
I’ll get to those next time when I get back to my normal routine.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.100
Thanks for watching.

